; SP16 Task 5 fixture - PARSE 1 quoted strings (B21), INPUT stream
; selection (B22) and the CHANCE fairness rate after the RNG
; replacement. Driven by tests/parser/scripts/parse1probe/probe.json
; through the differential harness, so every check below is answered by
; BOTH interpreters and compared: jDAAD implements PARSE 1 and INPUT
; already, which is what makes it evidence rather than self-assertion.
;
; The header line printed every loop carries all the state the checks
; need, so a screen read and a flag read say the same thing:
;   V= / N=    flags 33/34, the CURRENT logical sentence
;   QV= / QN=  flags 110/111, the sentence PARSE 1 built from the
;              quoted section - 254/254 means "no quoted section", the
;              markers written before PARSE 1 runs
;   C=         flag 100, CHANCE 50 successes out of 200 draws
;
; Loop shape note: every path ends in REDO. Falling off the end of
; process 0 ends the game on the original ZX interpreter (measured -
; see .superpowers/sdd/sp16-adjudications/zxadj.dsf), and while
; NextDAAD and jDAAD both re-push process 0 instead, there is no reason
; for a fixture to depend on that.
/CTL
_
/VOC
GO     10  verb
SAY    40  verb
DICE   41  verb
WIN    42  verb
NORTH  2   noun
HELLO  70  noun
WORLD  71  noun
/STX
/0  "It's too dark to see anything."
/1  "I can also see:"
/2  "What now?"
/3  "What next?"
/4  "What should I do now?"
/5  "What should I do next?"
/6  "I didn't understand."
/7  "I can't go in that direction."
/8  "I can't do that."
/9  "I have with me:"
/10 "I am wearing:"
/11 " "
/12 "Are you sure?"
/13 "Another go?"
/14 " "
/15 "OK."
/16 "Press any key."
/17 " "
/18 " "
/19 " "
/20 " "
/21 " "
/22 " "
/23 "I'm not wearing one of those."
/24 "I can't. My hands are full."
/25 "I already have the _."
/26 "There isn't one of those here."
/27 "I can't carry any more things."
/28 "I don't have one of those."
/29 "I'm already wearing the _."
/30 "Y"
/31 "N"
/32 "More..."
/33 ">"
/34 " "
/35 " "
/36 "I now have the _."
/37 "I'm now wearing the _."
/38 "I've removed the _."
/39 "I've dropped the _."
/40 "I can't wear the _."
/41 "I can't remove the _."
/42 "I can't remove the _. My hands are full."
/43 "The _ weighs too much for me."
/44 "The _ is in the"
/45 "The _ isn't in the"
/46 ", "
/47 " and "
/48 ".#n"
/49 "I don't have the _."
/50 "I'm not wearing the _."
/51 "."
/52 "There isn't one of those in the"
/53 "Nothing."
/54 " "
/55 " "
/56 " "
/57 "I/O Error"
/58 " "
/59 "File name error."
/60 "Type in name of file."
/MTX
/0 "SP16 T5 PROBE#n"
/OTX
/0 "a stone"
/LTX
/0 "Arena."
/CON
/0
/OBJ
/0    0   1   _ _  _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _   NORTH _
/PRO 0
> _ _   CLS
        MESSAGE 0
        MES "V="
        PRINT 33
        MES " N="
        PRINT 34
        MES " QV="
        PRINT 110
        MES " QN="
        PRINT 111
        MES " C="
        PRINT 100
        NEWLINE
> _ _   PARSE 0
        MES "NOPARSE#n"
> _ _   PROCESS 1
> _ _   REDO
/PRO 1
; B21. The two LETs run BEFORE the PARSE 1, so 254/254 is what survives
; when there was no quoted section - PARSE 1 passes as a condition then,
; the entry runs on and clears the in-SAY marker, and the copy below is
; skipped. When there WAS a quoted section PARSE 1 fails as a condition,
; this entry aborts ON it with the marker still set, and the next entry
; copies the sentence PARSE 1 built.
;
; The marker (flag 112) is why the second entry is headed "_ _" and not
; "SAY _": PARSE 1 REWRITES flag 33, so by the time control reaches the
; next entry the verb is no longer SAY and a SAY-headed entry would
; never match. That cost one full differential run to notice.
> SAY _   LET 110 254
          LET 111 254
          LET 112 1
          PARSE 1
          LET 112 0
          DONE
> _ _     EQ 112 1
          LET 112 0
          COPYFF 33 110
          COPYFF 34 111
          DONE
; CHANCE fairness. 200 independent draws of CHANCE 50 in process 2,
; counted into flag 100. Both legs draw from the same pinned seed
; through the same generator (the Z80 xorshift and its mirrors), so the
; count must be IDENTICAL on the two legs, and near 100 on both.
> DICE _  LET 100 0
          PROCESS 2
          DONE
; B22. Point flag 41 at window 2 and give window 2 a distinct place on
; the screen, so the next prompt and its input editor visibly move
; there and come back afterwards.
> WIN _   WINDOW 2
          WINAT 20 0
          WINSIZE 6 40
          WINDOW 0
          INPUT 2 0
          DONE
/PRO 2
> _ _   CHANCE 50
        PLUS 100 1
> _ _   CHANCE 50
        PLUS 100 1
